**assistant**:
> The `hidden()` function from the `shinyjs` package is a great way to initially hide UI elements such as buttons, but it’s crucial to make sure everything is set up correctly for it to work. Here's a checklist to troubleshoot why your button might not be initially hidden:
> 
> 1. **Ensure you have included the `shinyjs` package in your app.** You need to have `useShinyjs()` in your UI definition:
>    
>    ```r
>    library(shiny)
>    library(shinyjs)
>    
>    ui <- fluidPage(
>      useShinyjs(),
>      hidden(actionButton("saveButton", "Save Investuration"))
>    )
>    ```
> 
> 2. **Check for any JavaScript conflicts.** Ensure that no other JavaScript code in your app or included libraries is conflicting with `shinyjs`.
> 
> 3. **Verify browser support and settings.** Make sure your browser supports JavaScript and it is not disabled. Sometimes browser extensions can interfere, so testing in an incognito window or a different browser might help.
> 
> 4. **Inspect the page.** Use the browser developer tools (right-click on the page and choose "Inspect") to see if the button has the `display: none;` style applied. This could help in diagnosing CSS-related issues.
> 
> If you have verified all of the above and the button is still displaying, try creating a minimal reproducible example and run it as a new app. If it works, then incrementally bring in parts of your original app to identify what's causing the button to not be hidden.
